WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.320
So, for the fourth day of Christmas

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:17.220
we have four different drink receptacles.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.040
First, we have a mug from Russia

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.560
which is part of a project on supercritical fluids,

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:25.140
my area of research.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:28.980
Then you probably know I collect plastic water bottles

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.540
and Sam Tang just brought me this one from Hong Kong

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:35.920
new sort of water bottle from Hong Kong

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.440
they are usually green

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.840
Now this is a blue Watson's water bottle

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.320
Brady, who doesn't really like my water bottles, brought

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.540
me this one from San Francisco, where he films Numberphile

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:48.840
- his maths channel

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:51.380
and if you turn it round, you can see it says

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.280
BOXED WATER IS BETTER

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.640
I don't agree with him.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.140
And then finally, there is a vodka glass

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:02.900
which I really quite like because it's got my surname,

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.780
Poliakov, on it, though it's not spelled quite the same way

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:10.200
Some of you may have seen our video on Vodka

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:14.320
Mendeleyev vodka and of course, Poliakov vodka

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.020
So watch it if you haven't.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:19.860
So for our fourth day of Christmas

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.020
we have four drink receptacles

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:26.780
three chemical badges

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.020
two periodic table bed covers

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:33.640
and a piece of tartaric acid from a Swiss wine barrel

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.300
Let's see what happens for Day 5.

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.700
[Dr. Holly Krieger]
 ...this is known as the Mersenne Sequence.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.260
Well people have heard of the Mersenne Sequence because this famous question that we don't know

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:53.420
how to answer is, "Are there infinitely many
prime numbers in this sequence?"

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:57.920
Since this question is so hard, one 
easier question we could ask would be,

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.240
"What do the prime divisors of each 
element of this sequence look like?"

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.760
[Brady Haran] That seems like a harder question!

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.060
[Dr. Krieger, laughing] Well it might be a harder 
question depending on how specific you want to get.

